{
  "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
  "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:39 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michael J. Gottlieb (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question in this case is whether a district court is bound to accept a foreign sovereign's statement of foreign law in the unusual case in which the court has concerns about the statement's clarity, its completeness, or its consistency."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: May I ask you a question about your bottom line?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One of them relates to how much weight or deference a federal court should have given to a submission like the Ministry's amicus brief in this case, and that's the question that the parties have really focused on."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: All right. So -- so -- so what are -- look, what do you want? What words do you want to appear in the opinion? I mean, you're representing the State Department, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ministry of Commerce of the People's Republic of China",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First of all, Mr. Gottlieb suggested that the kind of program, Justice Breyer, that you described, the -- the price verification and chop, didn't actually operate that way, that there was some gap, and he cited a couple of pages in the Joint Appendix."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What do you do with the representation of China to the World Trade Organization that it had given up export administration of Vitamin C in 2001 and that it was a matter of voluntary agreement, with no coercion on the part of the government, with no government intervention?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Well, what should we\nsay in general? I just want you to reach that,\nbecause I can see three possible things. Say\nthey're like a state. I'm sorry, if Texas'\nSupreme Court says this is the law of Texas, I\ndon't care what somebody else says, that is\nwhat the law of Texas is, whether they held the\nexact opposite yesterday or not.\nAnother possibility: Chevron.\nMR. PHILLIPS: No.\nJUSTICE BREYER: Okay. Okay. Another\npossibility: Skidmore. You see? We take it\nfor the power to persuade but not the power to\n-- you know, it doesn't have the power to -- we\njust take it for what -- what it's worth and we\nshow respectful consideration.\nAnd maybe we could limit it to the\ninstance where it's the highest interpretive\nauthority of the state, or nearly that, and\ninstances where there are four professors\ngetting into an argument about it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Does the Supreme People's Court deal with cases like this? Isn't it true that, in most commercial matters, the courts are not used but arbitrators are?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. JACOBSON",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. JACOBSON: It's the -- the principle that the rule-maker has the authority to interpret its own rules."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2017-16-1220",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d5e039b39a25b32f432787828ee49a197ef5e48c472d5c18c524e23b073a491f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d5e039b39a25-0003-0008",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 72,
        "start": 72
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 12:40 p.m., the case was submitted.)"
    },
